    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "South Willard was originally named Willow Creek because of the numerous flowers located around the creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.intermountainhistories.org/items/show/368"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Willard was originally a fort used to fend off local Native American groups, including the dominant Shoshone.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.intermountainhistories.org/items/show/368"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Over thirty pioneer rock homes are still standing in Willard, many of which are on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.willardcityut.gov/history"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The economy of Willard historically centered around agriculture, with fruit crops serving as the major product.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.willardcityut.gov/history"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "By 1918, Willard became a station on the main line of the Oregon Short Line Railroad, which helped increase fruit exports.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
